    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D11", "U4"],
      "pins": ["D11.DIN", "U4 (no matching pin)"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet the WS2812B status LED D11 has its data input (DIN) wired to a short wire that terminates in the global label NET0004. Tracing every global label on the ESP32-C3-MINI-1 (U4) shows the MCU carries NET0002, NET0003, NET0006, NET0007, NET0009, NET0010, NET0012, NET0013, NET0014, NET0018 and NET0019, but NET0004 appears nowhere on U4 (and nowhere else in the design). The LED data line is therefore a one-member net with no driver.",
      "expected": "The specification states the board drives 'a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)' and the firmware (led.rs) sets its color per run state, so D11.DIN must be connected to an ESP32 GPIO. The reference design's D1 data line is MCU-driven; here it is left floating.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: 'D11 WS2812B' with 'DIN' pin wired to global label 'NET0004'; U4 ESP32-C3-MINI-1 right-side labels are NET0003/NET0009/NET0007/NET0014/NET0013/NET0019/NET0002/NET0010/NET0006 and left-side NET0018/NET0012 — NET0004 is absent."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Exposes status over Wi-Fi via a small webserver ... and a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The WS2812B status LED receives no data signal, so it will not light or will show undefined/frozen colors; the board loses its on-board run-state indication that the firmware is written to drive.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Power, arbitration, buck regulation, the heater high-side switch, the three MAX31855 SPI channels and the two fan-tach inputs all match the specification (only reference designators differ, which is not an anomaly). The one deviation the drawing supports is that the WS2812B status LED's data line (NET0004) is not connected to any ESP32 GPIO, leaving the status LED undriven."
}

